Pandas is the undisputed heavyweight champion of data analysis in Python—the foundational library that made Python the go-to language for data science.
But architecturally, it has one fatal flaw for production systems: it expects to eat its entire meal in one single bite.

Technical Breakdown :-
At its core, pandas is an in-memory, single-threaded DataFrame library built on top of NumPy arrays. 
It provides a phenomenally intuitive API for filtering, joining, and aggregating tabular data.
However, you should NOT use pandas under three specific conditions:

Data Exceeds RAM: Pandas uses 'eager evaluation' and loads the entire dataset into memory. 
Furthermore, operations like joins often create massive intermediate copies. The rule of thumb is that your RAM must be 5x to 10x larger than your dataset. 
If you have 16GB of RAM, a 5GB CSV will likely crash your machine.

Highly Parallel Workloads: Because it relies on standard Python, pandas is bound by the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL).
It processes data sequentially on a single CPU core, completely ignoring the other 15 cores on your modern server.

Complex Enterprise ETL: Pandas lacks native lazy-evaluation (query optimization) and doesn't inherently support reading/writing partitioned data 
across a network (like interacting with HDFS or cloud object storage natively at scale).

Buisness Context :- 
For a data scientist prototyping a machine learning model on a 500MB extract, 
pandas is the perfect tool for fast, ad-hoc discovery. But for enterprise data engineering, baking pandas into a daily ETL pipeline is a ticking time bomb.
As the business organically grows and the daily data ingestion scales from megabytes to gigabytes, that pandas script will inevitably hit the server's memory 
ceiling and fail silently in the middle of the night, violating critical data SLAs.

Design Decision :- 
As a senior engineer, I actively strip pandas out of heavy production data pipelines. 
If a pipeline is processing terabytes of data, I rewrite the logic in PySpark to distribute the workload across a cluster with lazy evaluation.
If we are architecturally bound to a single physical machine but need to process 50GB of data, I pivot to Polars or DuckDB. Polars, written in Rust, uses lazy execution, query optimization,
and fully utilizes every CPU core to process data in streaming chunks, bypassing the pandas memory bottleneck entirely without needing a full Spark cluster.
